To implement the EC2 Auto-Reaper, you need to perform three main tasks: 
set up the permissions (IAM), 
create the logic (Lambda),
Schedule the trigger (EventBridge).

Step 1: Create the IAM Policy & Role
Lambda needs "permission to act." Without this, the code will fail with an Unauthorized error.
Open IAM Console: Navigate to IAM.
Create Policy:
Click Policies > Create policy.
Select the JSON tab and paste this:
{
    "Version": "2012-10-17",
    "Statement": [
        {
            "Effect": "Allow",
            "Action": [
                "ec2:DescribeInstances",
                "ec2:StopInstances",
                "logs:CreateLogGroup",
                "logs:CreateLogStream",
                "logs:PutLogEvents"
            ],
            "Resource": "*"
        }
    ]
}
Click Next, name it EC2ReaperPolicy, and click Create policy.
Create Role:
Click Roles > Create role.
Select AWS Service and choose Lambda.
Search for your new EC2ReaperPolicy and check the box.
Name the role EC2ReaperRole and click Create role.

Step 2: Create the Lambda Function
Open Lambda Console: Navigate to Lambda.
Create Function:
import boto3

def lambda_handler(event, context):
    ec2 = boto3.client('ec2')
    
    # 1. Find all running instances
    instances = ec2.describe_instances(
        Filters=[{'Name': 'instance-state-name', 'Values': ['running']}]
    )
    
    # Extract just the Instance IDs into a list
    instance_ids = []
    for reservation in instances['Reservations']:
        for instance in reservation['Instances']:
            instance_ids.append(instance['InstanceId'])

    # 2. Stop the instances if any are found
    if instance_ids:
        print(f"Stopping instances: {instance_ids}")
        ec2.stop_instances(InstanceIds=instance_ids)
    else:
        print("No running instances found.")

    return {
        "status": "Success",
        "stopped_count": len(instance_ids)
    }

Choose Author from scratch.

Name: EC2InstanceReaper.

Runtime: Python 3.12 (or latest).

Permissions: Expand "Change default execution role," select Use an existing role, and pick the EC2ReaperRole you just made.

Click Create function.

Deploy Code:

In the Code tab, delete the default code and paste the Python script from the sre_project_guide.md file.

Click Deploy (crucial—changes won't take effect until you click this).

Adjust Timeout:

Go to Configuration > General configuration > Edit.

Change Timeout to 1 minute. Stopping multiple instances can take longer than the default 3 seconds.

Step 3: Schedule the Trigger (EventBridge)
Open EventBridge Console: Navigate to EventBridge.

Create Schedule:

Go to Scheduler > Schedules > Create schedule.

Schedule name: Every30MinsReaper.

Occurrence: Recurring schedule.

Schedule type: Rate-based schedule.

Value: 30 Unit: minutes.

Click Next.

Select Target:

Choose Templated targets > AWS Lambda Invoke.

Select your EC2InstanceReaper function.

Click Next through the settings and then Create schedule.

How to Test It
Manual Test: Inside the Lambda console, click the Test tab. Create a new event (the default JSON is fine) and click Test. Check the "Execution results" to see if it found any instances to stop.

Live Test: Launch a test EC2 instance. Wait 3 hours (or temporarily change limit_hours = 0.01 in the code for a 30-second test) and verify that EventBridge triggers the Lambda and stops the instance.

Update the Role trust relationship:
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "Service": [
          "lambda.amazonaws.com",
          "scheduler.amazonaws.com"
        ]
      },
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
